DSP.mod(8,{"m8-preprocessing":{"id":"m8-preprocessing","slug":"data-preprocessing-missing-values-outliers-encoding-scaling","m":8,"title":"Data preprocessing: missing values, outliers, encoding, scaling","min":40,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m7-leakage"],"tags":["feature-engineering","preprocessing"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Practical data preprocessing: types of missingness and imputation options, outlier detection and treatment, encoding categorical variables, scaling, and transformations, all fitted without leakage.","keyTerms":["missing values","MCAR MAR MNAR","imputation","outliers","winsorizing","one-hot encoding","target encoding","scaling","log transform"],"interviewQs":["How do you handle missing values? It depends on what?","One-hot vs target encoding: when and what are the risks?","Which models need feature scaling?","How do you deal with a categorical feature with 5,000 levels?"],"mistakes":["Fitting imputers and scalers on the full dataset","Target encoding without out-of-fold computation and smoothing","Deleting outliers without investigating them"],"practice":"Build a ColumnTransformer that imputes, scales numeric columns and one-hot encodes categoricals, and cross-validate it.","related":["m7-leakage","m8-feature-engineering","m3-eda"],"points":"Missing values\n\n- Types: MCAR (random), MAR (depends on observed), MNAR (depends on the missing value itself).\n- Options: drop rows/columns (if few), mean/median/mode, constant + missing indicator flag, group-wise imputation, KNN imputer, iterative (MICE), model-based, leave NaN for GBMs.\n- Always fit imputers on train only.\n\nOutliers\n\n- Detect: IQR, z-score, robust z (MAD), isolation forest, domain rules.\n- Treat: investigate first; cap/winsorize, transform (log), robust scaling/models, or separate model.\n\nEncoding categoricals\n\nMethod - When - Caveat\nOne-hot - Low cardinality, linear/NN models - Dimension blowup; drop one to avoid dummy trap in linear models\nOrdinal/label - Ordered categories; tree models for ids - Implies order for linear models\nFrequency/count encoding - High cardinality - Collisions in meaning\nTarget/mean encoding - High cardinality - Leakage; use out-of-fold + smoothing\nHashing - Very high cardinality, streaming - Collisions\nEmbeddings - Deep learning - Needs data\nWeight of Evidence (WoE) - Credit scorecards - Needs binning and monotonic checks\n\nScaling\n\n- Standardization (z-score): mean 0, SD 1; good for most models and PCA.\n- Min-Max: [0,1]; for NN/bounded inputs, sensitive to outliers.\n- Robust scaler: median/IQR; outlier-tolerant.\n- Needed for: KNN, SVM, k-means, linear/logistic with regularization, NN, PCA. Not needed for: trees/forests/boosting.\n\nTransformations: log/log1p (right skew), sqrt, Box-Cox/Yeo-Johnson, binning, clipping, polynomial/interactions."},"intuitive":"<p>Raw data is messy: there are blanks, weird extremes, text labels and numbers on wildly different scales. Preprocessing is tidying up so a model can learn: fill the blanks sensibly (and remember which were blank), check whether the extreme values are real or typos, turn words like &quot;Delhi&quot; into numbers in a way that does not invent an order, and put numbers on similar scales so no single column shouts over the others. The golden rule is to learn all the tidy-up rules from the training data only.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Preprocessing choices move model performance as much as the algorithm, and they are a common source of leakage.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Understand why data is missing or extreme, encode categories carefully, scale when the model needs it, and fit everything on train only.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Preprocessing turns raw data into something a model can use. Missing values: first ask why they are missing; then drop, impute with the median or a model, and add a missing flag, since missingness can itself be informative. Outliers: check if they are errors or real, then cap, transform or leave them for robust models. Categorical encoding: one-hot for few categories, ordinal for ordered ones, target or frequency encoding for high cardinality, with care to avoid leakage. Scaling: standardise or min-max for distance and gradient-based models; trees do not need it. The catch: every statistic, such as the mean to impute or the scaler&#39;s parameters, must be learned on training data only.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Fit on training data, apply to test data","html":"<p>Training ages: 20, 30, 40 and one missing value. One test customer is 50.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Impute with a statistic learned from training only.</strong> The training median is 30, so the missing age becomes 30. Training ages are now 20, 30, 40, 30.</li>\n<li><strong>Learn the scaling from training only.</strong> Mean = 30; standard deviation = 7.07.</li>\n<li><strong>Scale the training rows:</strong> (20 − 30) / 7.07 = −1.41; 30 gives 0; 40 gives +1.41; the imputed 30 gives 0.</li>\n<li><strong>Apply the same numbers to the test row:</strong> (50 − 30) / 7.07 = <strong>+2.83</strong>. The test customer is clearly older than anyone in training, and the model sees that honestly.</li>\n<li><strong>The leaky version.</strong> If you had scaled before splitting, using all five ages (mean 34, standard deviation 10.2), the test value would be (50 − 34) / 10.2 = +1.57. The test customer has quietly shaped the scaling, so evaluation is no longer a fair rehearsal of the future.</li>\n<li><strong>Make it automatic:</strong> put the imputer and scaler in a <code>Pipeline</code>, so they are fitted inside each training fold and only applied to validation and test data.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Every number used to prepare data (medians, means, category lists) is part of the model and must be learned from the training data alone.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-missing-values","text":"Missing values","level":3},{"id":"h-outliers","text":"Outliers","level":3},{"id":"h-encoding-categorical-variables","text":"Encoding categorical variables","level":3},{"id":"h-scaling","text":"Scaling","level":3},{"id":"h-transformations","text":"Transformations","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Missing values</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Types: MCAR (random), MAR (depends on observed), MNAR (depends on the missing value itself).</li>\n<li>Options: drop rows/columns (if few), mean/median/mode, constant + <strong>missing indicator flag</strong>, group-wise imputation, KNN imputer, iterative (MICE), model-based, leave NaN for GBMs.</li>\n<li>Always fit imputers on train only.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Outliers</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Detect: IQR, z-score, robust z (MAD), isolation forest, domain rules.</li>\n<li>Treat: investigate first; cap/winsorize, transform (log), robust scaling/models, or separate model.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Encoding categoricals</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>When</th><th>Caveat</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One-hot</td><td>Low cardinality, linear/NN models</td><td>Dimension blowup; drop one to avoid dummy trap in linear models</td></tr><tr><td>Ordinal/label</td><td>Ordered categories; tree models for ids</td><td>Implies order for linear models</td></tr><tr><td>Frequency/count encoding</td><td>High cardinality</td><td>Collisions in meaning</td></tr><tr><td>Target/mean encoding</td><td>High cardinality</td><td><strong>Leakage</strong>; use out-of-fold + smoothing</td></tr><tr><td>Hashing</td><td>Very high cardinality, streaming</td><td>Collisions</td></tr><tr><td>Embeddings</td><td>Deep learning</td><td>Needs data</td></tr><tr><td>Weight of Evidence (WoE)</td><td>Credit scorecards</td><td>Needs binning and monotonic checks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Scaling</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Standardization</strong> (z-score): mean 0, SD 1; good for most models and PCA.</li>\n<li><strong>Min-Max:</strong> [0,1]; for NN/bounded inputs, sensitive to outliers.</li>\n<li><strong>Robust scaler:</strong> median/IQR; outlier-tolerant.</li>\n<li>Needed for: KNN, SVM, k-means, linear/logistic with regularization, NN, PCA. Not needed for: trees/forests/boosting.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Transformations:</strong> log/log1p (right skew), sqrt, Box-Cox/Yeo-Johnson, binning, clipping, polynomial/interactions.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-missing-values\">Missing values</h3>\n<p><strong>Why is it missing?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MCAR</strong> (missing completely at random): unrelated to anything (a sensor glitch). Dropping rows is unbiased but wasteful.</li>\n<li><strong>MAR</strong> (missing at random): depends on <em>other observed</em> columns (younger users skip the income field). Impute using those columns.</li>\n<li><strong>MNAR</strong> (missing not at random): depends on the missing value itself (high earners hide income). The missingness is informative; add a <strong>missing indicator</strong> and be cautious.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Options</strong>, from simplest to richest:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Drop rows / columns</td><td>very few missing, or a column is mostly empty</td></tr><tr><td>Mean / median / mode</td><td>quick baseline; median for skewed data</td></tr><tr><td>Constant + <strong>missing-indicator flag</strong></td><td>when missingness carries signal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Group-wise</strong> imputation (median per city)</td><td>values depend on group</td></tr><tr><td>KNN imputer, <strong>iterative (MICE)</strong>, model-based</td><td>complex relationships; more compute</td></tr><tr><td>Leave NaN</td><td>gradient-boosting libraries handle it natively</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.impute <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> SimpleImputer\nimp = SimpleImputer(strategy=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;median&quot;</span>, add_indicator=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>)      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># adds a column flagging which values were imputed</span>\nX_train_imp = imp.fit_transform(X_train)                        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># learn the medians from TRAIN only</span>\nX_test_imp = imp.transform(X_test)                              <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># reuse them on test</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-outliers\">Outliers</h3>\n<p><strong>Detect:</strong> IQR fences (Q1 − 1.5·IQR, Q3 + 1.5·IQR), z-scores (|z| &gt; 3), <strong>robust z</strong> using the median and MAD (for skewed data), Isolation Forest, domain rules (age &gt; 120 is impossible). <strong>Treat:</strong> <em>investigate first</em>. Real but extreme values (a genuinely large order) carry information; errors (a typo, a unit mix-up) should be fixed or removed. Options: <strong>cap/winsorise</strong> at percentiles (for example P1/P99), apply a <strong>log transform</strong>, use <strong>robust models or scaling</strong>, or model the extremes separately.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-encoding-categorical-variables\">Encoding categorical variables</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How</th><th>Good for</th><th>Caveat</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>One-hot</strong></td><td>a 0/1 column per level</td><td>low cardinality, linear/NN models</td><td>dimension blow-up; drop one level (dummy trap) for linear models</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ordinal / label</strong></td><td>integers</td><td>truly ordered levels (S &lt; M &lt; L); tree models for IDs</td><td>implies a false order for linear models</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Frequency / count</strong></td><td>replace by how often it occurs</td><td>high cardinality</td><td>different categories with equal counts collide</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Target (mean) encoding</strong></td><td>replace by the mean target per level</td><td>high cardinality</td><td><strong>leakage</strong> unless computed <strong>out-of-fold with smoothing</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hashing</strong></td><td>hash into a fixed number of buckets</td><td>huge or streaming vocabularies</td><td>collisions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Embeddings</strong></td><td>learned dense vectors</td><td>deep learning</td><td>needs data</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Weight of Evidence</strong></td><td>log(share of goods / share of bads) per bin</td><td>credit scorecards</td><td>needs binning and monotonicity checks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Smoothed target encoding.</strong> A level seen 5 times with a 0.8 mean target should not be trusted fully. Blend with the global mean (0.3) using a pseudo-count of 10: <code>(5 × 0.8 + 10 × 0.3) / (5 + 10) = 0.47</code>. Compute it <strong>out-of-fold</strong> so a row&#39;s own label never influences its own feature.</p>\n<p><strong>WoE and IV example</strong> (three income bins, credit default): goods 300/500/200 and bads 50/30/120. The share of goods in the first bin is 0.30 and of bads 0.25, so WoE = ln(0.30/0.25) = 0.18; the mid bin has WoE 1.20 (safe) and the high-risk bin −1.10. <strong>Information Value</strong> = Σ (share_good − share_bad) × WoE = 0.87: a very strong predictor (rule of thumb: IV above 0.3 strong, below 0.02 useless, and suspiciously high values hint at leakage).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-scaling\">Scaling</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Scaler</th><th>Formula</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Standardisation</strong></td><td>(x − mean) / SD</td><td>default for most models and PCA</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Min-max</strong></td><td>(x − min) / (max − min) in [0, 1]</td><td>neural nets, bounded inputs; sensitive to outliers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Robust</strong></td><td>(x − median) / IQR</td><td>outlier-tolerant</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Needed for:</strong> KNN, SVM, k-means, PCA, regularised linear/logistic regression, neural nets, gradient descent generally. <strong>Not needed for:</strong> trees, random forests, gradient boosting (splits depend on order only).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-transformations\">Transformations</h3>\n<p><code>log1p</code> for right-skewed positive data (income, counts); <code>sqrt</code>; <strong>Box-Cox / Yeo-Johnson</strong> (find the best power transform automatically; Yeo-Johnson allows zero and negatives); binning; clipping; polynomial and interaction terms.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m8-preprocessing~1","q":"A feature <code>city</code> has 5,000 distinct values and you want to use a linear model. What do you do?","a":"<p>One-hot would create 5,000 sparse columns (overfitting, memory). Options: (1) <strong>group rare levels</strong> into &quot;other&quot;, or roll cities up into regions or tiers; (2) <strong>out-of-fold smoothed target encoding</strong> (or weight of evidence) so each city becomes one numeric value without leaking the label; (3) <strong>frequency encoding</strong> if popularity is predictive; (4) <strong>hashing</strong> if the vocabulary is unbounded; (5) learned <strong>embeddings</strong> in a neural model; or use <strong>CatBoost</strong>, which handles high-cardinality categoricals natively. Validate that the encoding is computed inside cross-validation.</p>\n","s":"Group rare cities, use out-of-fold smoothed target or frequency encoding, or embeddings; avoid plain one-hot."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-preprocessing~ca0_1","q":"A &#39;city&#39; column has 5,000 distinct values. How do you encode it?","a":"<p>Not one-hot (too wide). Use frequency or target encoding computed out-of-fold, hashing, or an embedding.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-preprocessing~ca0_2","q":"Which models need scaled features and which do not?","a":"<p>Need it: KNN, SVM, linear/logistic regression with regularisation, neural nets, PCA. Do not: decision trees, random forests, boosting.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-preprocessing~ca0_3","q":"Why add a &#39;was_missing&#39; flag when imputing?","a":"<p>Missingness often carries signal (for example no income reported); imputing alone hides it.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m8-preprocessing~ta1_1","q":"<p>You one-hot encode <code>city</code> using the training data, which has Pune, Delhi and Mumbai. In production a customer from Jaipur arrives. What happens with a default encoder, and how do you handle it?</p>\n","a":"<p>A strict encoder raises an error for an unseen category, or the columns no longer match the model&#39;s inputs. Use <code>OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=&quot;ignore&quot;)</code>, so Jaipur becomes all zeros (none of the known cities), or group rare and unseen cities into an &quot;other&quot; bucket during training. Then monitor how often unseen categories appear: a sudden rise is a sign of drift.</p>\n"}]},"m8-feature-engineering":{"id":"m8-feature-engineering","slug":"feature-engineering-and-feature-selection","m":8,"title":"Feature engineering and feature selection","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m8-preprocessing"],"tags":["feature-engineering","feature-selection"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Feature engineering ideas with examples (time, aggregates, ratios, text, geo, lag) and feature selection methods (filter, wrapper, embedded), with leakage checks.","keyTerms":["datetime features","cyclical encoding","aggregates","RFM","ratios","interactions","lag features","filter wrapper embedded selection","mutual information"],"interviewQs":["What features would you engineer for a food-delivery ETA model?","How do you encode the hour of day?","Filter vs wrapper vs embedded feature selection?"],"mistakes":["Creating features using future information","Selecting features on the full dataset before cross-validation","Adding hundreds of features without checking stability"],"practice":"For a churn problem, list ten features across recency, frequency, monetary, support and product usage, and mark the prediction-time availability of each.","related":["m8-preprocessing","m7-leakage","m9-time-series"],"points":"- Datetime: hour, day of week, month, is_weekend, is_holiday (Indian holidays/festivals), days since event, cyclical encoding (sin/cos for hour/month).\n- Aggregates: user-level count, sum, mean, std over last 7/30/90 days; recency, frequency, monetary.\n- Ratios: debt-to-income, orders per month, cart-to-purchase ratio.\n- Text: length, word counts, TF-IDF, embeddings, keyword flags.\n- Geo: city tier, pin-code clusters, distance between points (haversine).\n- Interactions: price x quantity; domain-based crosses.\n- Lag/rolling features for time series (with strict time-awareness).\n- Target leakage check for each engineered feature: \"Would I have this at prediction time?\"\n\n- Filter: correlation, chi-square, mutual information, variance threshold, ANOVA F, IV (credit).\n- Wrapper: RFE (recursive feature elimination), forward/backward selection.\n- Embedded: L1 regularization, tree importances.\n- Dimensionality reduction: PCA (unsupervised, loses interpretability).\n- Watch: multicollinearity, stability across folds, permutation importance on validation."},"intuitive":"<p>Models only know the columns you give them, so good features are clues you craft. &quot;Day of week&quot; and &quot;is it a festival?&quot; may explain orders better than a raw timestamp. &quot;Orders in the last 30 days&quot; says more about a customer than their ID. Ratios such as &quot;debt divided by income&quot; compress two numbers into one meaningful one. Afterwards, feature selection removes clues that are useless or repeat each other. The safety question to ask of every clue: &quot;Will I actually know this at the moment I have to predict?&quot;</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Feature engineering is where domain knowledge becomes model performance, and interviews test it with &quot;what features would you build?&quot;.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Build informative, time-safe features; then prune the redundant ones.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Feature engineering means creating inputs that make the pattern easier to learn. Examples are dates split into day of week and month, ratios like spend per visit, aggregates like a user&#39;s average order in the last thirty days, interaction terms, log transforms for skewed values and bins for thresholds. Domain knowledge is the best source. Feature selection then removes noise: filter methods score features by correlation or mutual information, wrapper methods like recursive elimination search subsets, and embedded methods like lasso or tree importance select during training. The catch: any aggregate must be computed using only data available before the prediction time, or it leaks the future.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Build features from raw orders, as of a date","html":"<p>Predict on 1 April whether a customer will order in April. Her orders: 3 January (800), 20 February (1,200), 10 March (600), 25 March (900).</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Fix the as-of date: 1 April.</strong> Use only orders before it.</li>\n<li><strong>Recency:</strong> days since the last order = 1 April − 25 March = <strong>7 days</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Frequency:</strong> orders in the last 90 days (since 2 January) = <strong>4</strong>; in the last 30 days (since 2 March) = <strong>2</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Monetary:</strong> total spend in the last 90 days = 800 + 1,200 + 600 + 900 = <strong>3,500</strong>; average order value = <strong>875</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Trend:</strong> 2 orders in the last 30 days against 4 in 90, so her monthly rate is roughly steady (2 per month against an average of 1.3), slightly rising.</li>\n<li><strong>The leak to avoid.</strong> Do not compute &quot;average order value over the whole year&quot;, because it would include April, the very period you are predicting.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Value</th><th>Plain meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>recency_days</td><td>7</td><td>bought very recently</td></tr><tr><td>orders_90d</td><td>4</td><td>a regular customer</td></tr><tr><td>orders_30d</td><td>2</td><td>currently active</td></tr><tr><td>aov_90d</td><td>875</td><td>basket size</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Good features summarise behaviour up to the prediction moment: how recently, how often, how much and which way it is moving.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-8-2","text":"8.2 Feature engineering ideas (be ready with examples)","level":2},{"id":"c-8-3","text":"8.3 Feature selection","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-idea-bank-with-examples","text":"Idea bank, with examples","level":3},{"id":"h-feature-selection","text":"Feature selection","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-8-2\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.2</span> Feature engineering ideas (be ready with examples)</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Datetime:</strong> hour, day of week, month, is_weekend, is_holiday (Indian holidays/festivals), days since event, cyclical encoding (sin/cos for hour/month).</li>\n<li><strong>Aggregates:</strong> user-level count, sum, mean, std over last 7/30/90 days; recency, frequency, monetary.</li>\n<li><strong>Ratios:</strong> debt-to-income, orders per month, cart-to-purchase ratio.</li>\n<li><strong>Text:</strong> length, word counts, TF-IDF, embeddings, keyword flags.</li>\n<li><strong>Geo:</strong> city tier, pin-code clusters, distance between points (haversine).</li>\n<li><strong>Interactions:</strong> price x quantity; domain-based crosses.</li>\n<li><strong>Lag/rolling features</strong> for time series (with strict time-awareness).</li>\n<li><strong>Target leakage check</strong> for each engineered feature: &quot;Would I have this at prediction time?&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-8-3\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.3</span> Feature selection</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Filter:</strong> correlation, chi-square, mutual information, variance threshold, ANOVA F, IV (credit).</li>\n<li><strong>Wrapper:</strong> RFE (recursive feature elimination), forward/backward selection.</li>\n<li><strong>Embedded:</strong> L1 regularization, tree importances.</li>\n<li><strong>Dimensionality reduction:</strong> PCA (unsupervised, loses interpretability).</li>\n<li>Watch: multicollinearity, stability across folds, permutation importance on validation.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-idea-bank-with-examples\">Idea bank, with examples</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Ideas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Datetime</strong></td><td>hour, day of week, month, is_weekend, <strong>is_holiday</strong> (Indian holidays and festivals such as Diwali, IPL match days), days since an event, <strong>cyclical encoding</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aggregates</strong></td><td>per user: count, sum, mean, std over the last 7/30/90 days; <strong>recency, frequency, monetary (RFM)</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ratios</strong></td><td>debt-to-income, orders per month, cart-to-purchase ratio</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Text</strong></td><td>length, word count, TF-IDF, embeddings, keyword flags</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Geo</strong></td><td>city tier, pin-code clusters, haversine distance between points</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interactions</strong></td><td>price × quantity; domain crosses (city × hour)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lags and rolling</strong> (time series)</td><td>yesterday&#39;s value, 7-day rolling mean (always time-aware)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Cyclical encoding.</strong> Hour 23 and hour 0 are adjacent, but numerically far apart. Encode the hour as a point on a circle:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;hour_sin&quot;</span>] = np.sin(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span> * np.pi * df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;hour&quot;</span>] / <span class=\"hljs-number\">24</span>)\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;hour_cos&quot;</span>] = np.cos(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span> * np.pi * df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;hour&quot;</span>] / <span class=\"hljs-number\">24</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># hour 0 -&gt; (0, 1); hour 6 -&gt; (1, 0); hour 12 -&gt; (0, -1); hour 23 -&gt; (-0.26, 0.97) which is close to hour 0</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>(Trees can use raw hour fine; this matters for linear and neural models.)</p>\n<p><strong>RFM features</strong> in pandas (as of a fixed date):</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">asof = pd.Timestamp(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-03-31&quot;</span>)\nrfm = orders.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>).agg(recency=(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda</span> d: (asof - d.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">max</span>()).days),\n                                    frequency=(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;nunique&quot;</span>), monetary=(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sum&quot;</span>))</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Example: food-delivery ETA.</strong> Distance (haversine), restaurant prep time history, time of day and day of week, is_raining, rider&#39;s recent average speed, orders currently assigned to the rider, zone congestion, item count, first-mile and last-mile distances, festival flag.</p>\n<p><strong>The leakage check for every feature:</strong> &quot;Would I have this value at prediction time, computed the same way?&quot;</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-feature-selection\">Feature selection</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Examples</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Filter</strong></td><td>correlation, chi-square, mutual information, ANOVA F, variance threshold, IV (credit)</td><td>fast, model-independent; ignores interactions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Wrapper</strong></td><td>RFE (recursive feature elimination), forward/backward selection</td><td>uses the model; expensive; risk of overfitting the selection</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Embedded</strong></td><td>L1 regularisation, tree importances</td><td>selection happens during training</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dimensionality reduction</strong></td><td>PCA</td><td>creates new combined features; loses interpretability</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.feature_selection <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> SelectKBest, mutual_info_classif, RFE\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.linear_model <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> LogisticRegression\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> Pipeline\npipe = Pipeline([(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select&quot;</span>, SelectKBest(mutual_info_classif, k=<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>)), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;clf&quot;</span>, LogisticRegression(max_iter=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span>))])   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># selection INSIDE the CV loop</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Watch for <strong>multicollinearity</strong> among selected features, <strong>stability</strong> of the selected set across folds, and use <strong>permutation importance on validation data</strong> to confirm a feature really helps.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m8-feature-engineering~1","q":"Design features for predicting whether a customer will churn in the next 30 days.","a":"<p>Fix a <strong>snapshot date</strong>; features use only data before it, the label looks 30 days ahead. Features: <strong>recency</strong> (days since last order), <strong>frequency</strong> (orders in the last 7/30/90 days) and its <strong>trend</strong> (last 30 days vs the previous 30), <strong>monetary</strong> (average order value, discount dependence), <strong>service quality</strong> (late deliveries, cancellations, refunds, support tickets and their sentiment), <strong>engagement</strong> (app opens, search-to-order ratio, cart abandonment), tenure, city tier, and competitor/season signals. Validate with a time-based split and check each feature&#39;s availability at scoring time.</p>\n","s":"Recency, frequency and trend of activity, support tickets, delivery problems, engagement and price sensitivity, all as of the snapshot date."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-feature-engineering~ca0_1","q":"Predicting churn: suggest three engineered features from an events table.","a":"<p>Days since last activity, number of sessions in the last 7 vs 30 days (trend), and support tickets raised; all computed as of the prediction date.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-feature-engineering~ca0_2","q":"Filter versus embedded feature selection?","a":"<p>Filter ranks features independently of the model (fast, ignores interactions); embedded selection happens inside training, as with L1 or tree importance.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-feature-engineering~ca0_3","q":"Why log-transform a skewed feature like income?","a":"<p>It compresses the long tail so extreme values do not dominate linear models, and makes relationships more linear.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m8-feature-engineering~ta1_1","q":"<p>You encode hour of day as <code>sin(2π·h/24)</code> and <code>cos(2π·h/24)</code> instead of the raw number 0 to 23. Why? Compare 23:00 with 00:00 in both encodings.</p>\n","a":"<p>As raw numbers, 23 and 0 are 23 apart, although they are one hour apart in reality. With the cyclic encoding, 23:00 is about (−0.26, 0.97) and 00:00 is (0, 1), a distance of about 0.26, the same as any other pair of adjacent hours. 12:00 is (0, −1), the farthest point from midnight. The two numbers place the hours on a clock face, so the model sees that the day wraps around.</p>\n"}]},"m8-dim-reduction":{"id":"m8-dim-reduction","slug":"dimensionality-reduction-pca-t-sne-umap-lda","m":8,"title":"Dimensionality reduction: PCA, t-SNE, UMAP, LDA","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":[],"tags":["dimensionality-reduction","pca"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Dimensionality reduction: how PCA works and how to choose components, when to use t-SNE or UMAP, and LDA as a supervised alternative.","keyTerms":["PCA","principal components","explained variance","scree plot","t-SNE","UMAP","LDA","standardization"],"interviewQs":["Explain PCA in simple terms and mathematically.","How many components do you keep?","Why is t-SNE not suitable as a preprocessing step?"],"mistakes":["Not standardising before PCA","Interpreting distances between t-SNE clusters literally","Using PCA when interpretability is required"],"practice":"Run PCA on a standardised dataset, plot cumulative explained variance, and choose components for 95%.","related":["m5-other","m9-clustering","m7-knn"],"points":"- PCA: projects data to orthogonal directions of maximum variance (eigenvectors of covariance matrix). Standardize first. Choose components by cumulative explained variance (e.g., 95%) or scree plot. Components are linear combinations, less interpretable.\n- t-SNE / UMAP: nonlinear, for visualization; do not interpret distances between clusters literally; t-SNE is not for feature inputs.\n- LDA (linear discriminant analysis): supervised; maximizes class separation."},"intuitive":"<p>Imagine photographing a long pencil. From the side you see its length; from the end you see a dot and lose almost everything. PCA finds the best angle to photograph your data from so that you keep as much of the &quot;spread&quot; as possible while using fewer dimensions. The first direction captures the most variation, the second captures the most of what is left (at right angles), and so on. You keep only the first few and throw away the rest, which compresses the data with little loss.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> High-dimensional data is slow, noisy and hard to visualise; PCA is the standard first tool.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> PCA keeps the directions of maximum variance; standardise first; choose components by explained variance.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Dimensionality reduction compresses many features into a few. PCA finds new axes, the principal components, along which the data varies most; each is a linear combination of the original features, and they are uncorrelated. I keep enough to explain, say, ninety-five per cent of variance, and I must scale features first. It helps with noise, speed and visualisation, at the cost of interpretability. t-SNE and UMAP are non-linear methods for plotting clusters in two dimensions; distances between clusters in a t-SNE plot are not meaningful, and they are not for feeding models. LDA is supervised and finds axes that best separate classes. The catch: PCA ignores the target, so top components may not be the predictive ones.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"How much does PCA compress two correlated features?","html":"<p>Two exam scores, both standardised, with correlation r = 0.9.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The picture.</strong> The points lie in a tight cigar shape along the diagonal, because students who score high on one test tend to score high on the other.</li>\n<li><strong>PC1</strong> points along the cigar (roughly &quot;overall ability&quot;); <strong>PC2</strong> points across it (&quot;better at test A than test B&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>The share of variance on each.</strong> For two standardised features, the components carry (1 + r) and (1 − r) units of variance out of 2. With r = 0.9: PC1 has 1.9 / 2 = <strong>95%</strong>, PC2 has <strong>5%</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Decision:</strong> keep PC1 only. One number now describes 95% of the variation in two.</li>\n<li><strong>The other case, r = 0.2:</strong> PC1 has 1.2 / 2 = 60%, PC2 has 40%. Dropping PC2 would throw away a lot; PCA does not help much when features are not correlated.</li>\n<li><strong>Why standardise first.</strong> If one score ran from 0 to 1,000 and the other from 0 to 10, PC1 would simply follow the large-scale feature, because PCA chases raw variance.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> PCA compresses well exactly when features are redundant (highly correlated). Read the explained-variance ratio before deciding how many components to keep.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-pca-step-by-step","text":"PCA, step by step","level":3},{"id":"h-t-sne-and-umap-for-visualisation","text":"t-SNE and UMAP: for visualisation","level":3},{"id":"h-lda-linear-discriminant-analysis","text":"LDA (linear discriminant analysis)","level":3},{"id":"h-when-to-use-which","text":"When to use which","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>PCA:</strong> projects data to orthogonal directions of maximum variance (eigenvectors of covariance matrix). Standardize first. Choose components by cumulative explained variance (e.g., 95%) or scree plot. Components are linear combinations, less interpretable.</li>\n<li><strong>t-SNE / UMAP:</strong> nonlinear, for visualization; do not interpret distances between clusters literally; t-SNE is not for feature inputs.</li>\n<li><strong>LDA (linear discriminant analysis):</strong> supervised; maximizes class separation.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-pca-step-by-step\">PCA, step by step</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Standardise</strong> each feature (otherwise large-unit features dominate the variance).</li>\n<li>Compute the <strong>covariance matrix</strong> of the features.</li>\n<li>Find its <strong>eigenvectors</strong> (directions, the principal components) and <strong>eigenvalues</strong> (the variance along each).</li>\n<li>Sort by eigenvalue, keep the top <code>k</code>, and <strong>project</strong> the data onto them.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The components are <strong>orthogonal</strong> linear combinations of the original features; each explains a share of the total variance.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.decomposition <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> PCA\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.preprocessing <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> StandardScaler\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> make_pipeline\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.datasets <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> load_iris\n\nX = load_iris().data\npca = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), PCA()).fit(X)\nratios = pca[-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>].explained_variance_ratio_\nratios.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">round</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>), ratios.cumsum().<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">round</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [0.73  0.229 0.037 0.005]   cumulative [0.73 0.959 0.995 1.0]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Two components keep 96% of the information of these four features: a great 2-D picture.</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>How many components?</strong> Keep enough for a target such as <strong>95% cumulative explained variance</strong> (<code>PCA(n_components=0.95)</code>), look for the <strong>elbow in the scree plot</strong>, or choose by the downstream model&#39;s validation score. Components are hard to interpret (mixtures of features) and PCA only captures <strong>linear</strong> structure; it is <strong>unsupervised</strong>, so the top-variance directions are not necessarily the most predictive ones.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-t-sne-and-umap-for-visualisation\">t-SNE and UMAP: for visualisation</h3>\n<p>Non-linear methods that preserve <strong>local neighbourhoods</strong> to draw 2-D maps of complex data such as embeddings. Cautions: distances <strong>between</strong> clusters and cluster sizes are not meaningful; results depend on hyperparameters (perplexity, <code>n_neighbors</code>) and randomness; <strong>t-SNE has no stable mapping for new data</strong>, so do not use it as feature preprocessing. UMAP is faster, preserves more global structure and can transform new points, but the same interpretation cautions apply.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-lda-linear-discriminant-analysis\">LDA (linear discriminant analysis)</h3>\n<p>A <strong>supervised</strong> linear method that finds directions maximising <strong>class separation</strong> (between-class variance relative to within-class variance). Useful for classification and for supervised dimensionality reduction (at most <code>classes − 1</code> components).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-when-to-use-which\">When to use which</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Tool</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Compress features, speed up, reduce noise</td><td>PCA (or truncated SVD for sparse data)</td></tr><tr><td>Visualise high-dimensional structure</td><td>UMAP or t-SNE</td></tr><tr><td>Reduce dimensions while keeping class separability</td><td>LDA</td></tr><tr><td>Keep interpretability</td><td>feature selection instead of PCA</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m8-dim-reduction~1","q":"After PCA on standardised data, the first component explains 73% of the variance. Does that mean it is the best feature for predicting your target?","a":"<p>Variance is not predictive power. The top component captures the direction with the most spread; a low-variance direction could separate your classes perfectly. Evaluate components with the downstream model (cross-validated), consider supervised reduction (LDA, PLS, feature selection), and do not assume high-variance means high-signal.</p>\n","s":"No: PCA maximises variance, not relevance to the target."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-dim-reduction~ca0_1","q":"Why standardise before PCA?","a":"<p>PCA maximises variance, so a feature with a large scale would dominate the first component regardless of importance.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-dim-reduction~ca0_2","q":"Can you read cluster sizes and distances off a t-SNE plot?","a":"<p>No: t-SNE preserves local neighbourhoods but not global distances or sizes; use it for exploration only.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-dim-reduction~ca0_3","q":"PCA versus LDA?","a":"<p>PCA is unsupervised and maximises variance; LDA is supervised and maximises class separation.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m8-dim-reduction~ta1_1","q":"<p>A PCA on 10 standardised features reports explained-variance ratios of 0.42, 0.21, 0.12, 0.08, 0.06, 0.04, 0.03, 0.02, 0.01, 0.01. How many components keep at least 90% of the variance, and what do you lose by using them in a model?</p>\n","a":"<p>The cumulative shares are 0.42, 0.63, 0.75, 0.83, 0.89, 0.93, and so on, so 6 components reach 93% (5 give 89%). You go from 10 features to 6 and keep most of the information, but each component mixes all 10 original features, so you can no longer say &quot;income matters&quot; from the model. And PCA ignores the target, so the discarded 7% could, in principle, hold the predictive signal.</p>\n"}]},"m8-classification-metrics":{"id":"m8-classification-metrics","slug":"classification-metrics","m":8,"title":"Classification metrics","min":40,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m7-logistic-regression"],"tags":["metrics","classification"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Classification metrics from the confusion matrix, with worked numbers, ROC vs PR curves, threshold effects, calibration, and credit-risk metrics.","keyTerms":["confusion matrix","accuracy","precision","recall","specificity","F1","F-beta","ROC-AUC","PR-AUC","log loss","calibration","KS","Gini","MCC"],"interviewQs":["Explain precision and recall with an example.","ROC-AUC vs PR-AUC: when does each mislead?","What are KS and Gini in credit modelling?","What does calibration mean and how do you fix it?"],"mistakes":["Reporting accuracy on imbalanced data","Using ROC-AUC alone with extreme imbalance","Comparing F1 scores at different thresholds"],"practice":"Compute a confusion matrix and all derived metrics for a fraud model on 1,000 transactions, then move the threshold and see what changes.","related":["m8-metric-choice","m8-imbalanced","m7-logistic-regression"],"points":"Predicted + - Predicted -\nActual + - TP - FN\nActual - - FP - TN\n\n- Accuracy = (TP+TN)/N. Misleading under imbalance.\n- Precision = TP/(TP+FP): \"of those flagged, how many are truly positive?\"\n- Recall / Sensitivity / TPR = TP/(TP+FN): \"of true positives, how many did we catch?\"\n- Specificity = TN/(TN+FP). FPR = 1 - specificity.\n- F1 = harmonic mean of precision and recall; F-beta weights recall more (beta > 1).\n- ROC-AUC: probability that a random positive ranks above a random negative; threshold-independent; can look optimistic under heavy imbalance.\n- PR-AUC / Average Precision: better under heavy imbalance.\n- Log loss: penalizes confident wrong probabilities; measures calibration and discrimination.\n- Brier score, calibration curve: are predicted probabilities trustworthy?\n- KS statistic, Gini (2*AUC - 1): common in Indian BFSI credit modeling.\n- Lift / gain / decile charts: marketing and targeting use cases.\n- MCC, Cohen's kappa: balanced single-number summaries.\n- Multi-class: macro (equal class weight), micro (global counts), weighted averages."},"intuitive":"<p>Suppose a smoke alarm has to spot real fires. <strong>Recall</strong> asks &quot;of all the real fires, how many did it catch?&quot;. <strong>Precision</strong> asks &quot;when it rings, how often is there really a fire?&quot;. A jumpy alarm catches every fire but cries wolf constantly (high recall, low precision); a sleepy one is almost always right when it rings but misses fires (high precision, low recall). Turning the sensitivity dial moves you along that trade-off. <strong>Accuracy</strong> can look great while the alarm is useless, because fires are rare.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Metric questions are in almost every ML interview, and choosing the wrong one is a classic red flag.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Understand the confusion matrix; choose metrics by business cost; beware accuracy on rare events.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Everything starts from the confusion matrix: true positives, false positives, true negatives, false negatives. Accuracy is the share correct, misleading with imbalance. Precision is of those I flagged, how many were right. Recall is of the real positives, how many I found. F1 is their harmonic mean. For example, with a thousand cases, one hundred fraud, and a model that finds sixty and flags a hundred, precision is sixty per cent and recall sixty per cent. ROC AUC measures ranking quality across thresholds; precision-recall AUC is more informative for rare positives. Log loss scores the probabilities themselves. The catch: each metric hides something, so I choose by the cost of each error type.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Move the threshold, watch precision and recall","html":"<p>Ten transactions, sorted by the model&#39;s fraud score. F = fraud (positive), L = legitimate. There are 5 frauds.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>score</th><th>0.95</th><th>0.90</th><th>0.80</th><th>0.70</th><th>0.60</th><th>0.45</th><th>0.40</th><th>0.30</th><th>0.20</th><th>0.10</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>truth</td><td>F</td><td>F</td><td>L</td><td>F</td><td>L</td><td>F</td><td>L</td><td>L</td><td>F</td><td>L</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Threshold 0.5</strong> (flag anything above it): 5 flagged, of which 3 are fraud. TP = 3, FP = 2, FN = 2. Precision = 3/5 = <strong>0.60</strong>; recall = 3/5 = <strong>0.60</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Threshold 0.35:</strong> 7 flagged, 4 fraud. TP = 4, FP = 3, FN = 1. Precision = 4/7 = <strong>0.57</strong>; recall = 4/5 = <strong>0.80</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Threshold 0.15:</strong> 9 flagged, all 5 frauds among them. TP = 5, FP = 4, FN = 0. Precision = 5/9 = <strong>0.56</strong>; recall = <strong>1.00</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Threshold</th><th>Flagged</th><th>Precision</th><th>Recall</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.50</td><td>5</td><td>0.60</td><td>0.60</td></tr><tr><td>0.35</td><td>7</td><td>0.57</td><td>0.80</td></tr><tr><td>0.15</td><td>9</td><td>0.56</td><td>1.00</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"4\">\n<li><strong>The trade-off.</strong> Lowering the threshold catches more fraud (recall rises) at the cost of more false alarms (precision falls).</li>\n<li><strong>The model did not change, only the decision.</strong> Precision and recall belong to a model <em>and</em> a threshold.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A classifier gives scores; you choose the threshold. Plot precision and recall across thresholds and choose with the business.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-8-5-classification-confusion-matrix","text":"Classification (confusion matrix)","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-confusion-matrix","text":"The confusion matrix","level":3},{"id":"h-threshold-free-summaries","text":"Threshold-free summaries","level":3},{"id":"h-indian-bfsi-favourites-ks-gini-lift","text":"Indian BFSI favourites: KS, Gini, lift","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-8-5-classification-confusion-matrix\">Classification (confusion matrix)</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Predicted +</th><th>Predicted -</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actual +</td><td>TP</td><td>FN</td></tr><tr><td>Actual -</td><td>FP</td><td>TN</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Accuracy</strong> = (TP+TN)/N. Misleading under imbalance.</li>\n<li><strong>Precision</strong> = TP/(TP+FP): &quot;of those flagged, how many are truly positive?&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Recall / Sensitivity / TPR</strong> = TP/(TP+FN): &quot;of true positives, how many did we catch?&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Specificity</strong> = TN/(TN+FP). <strong>FPR</strong> = 1 - specificity.</li>\n<li><strong>F1</strong> = harmonic mean of precision and recall; <strong>F-beta</strong> weights recall more (beta &gt; 1).</li>\n<li><strong>ROC-AUC:</strong> probability that a random positive ranks above a random negative; threshold-independent; can look optimistic under heavy imbalance.</li>\n<li><strong>PR-AUC / Average Precision:</strong> better under heavy imbalance.</li>\n<li><strong>Log loss:</strong> penalizes confident wrong probabilities; measures calibration and discrimination.</li>\n<li><strong>Brier score, calibration curve:</strong> are predicted probabilities trustworthy?</li>\n<li><strong>KS statistic, Gini (2*AUC - 1):</strong> common in Indian BFSI credit modeling.</li>\n<li><strong>Lift / gain / decile charts:</strong> marketing and targeting use cases.</li>\n<li><strong>MCC, Cohen&#39;s kappa:</strong> balanced single-number summaries.</li>\n<li><strong>Multi-class:</strong> macro (equal class weight), micro (global counts), weighted averages.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-confusion-matrix\">The confusion matrix</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Predicted +</th><th>Predicted −</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Actual +</strong></td><td>TP</td><td>FN</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Actual −</strong></td><td>FP</td><td>TN</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> 1,000 transactions, 20 fraudulent. A model flags 45 transactions: it catches 15 frauds (TP = 15), misses 5 (FN = 5), and flags 30 legitimate ones (FP = 30); TN = 950.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Formula</th><th>Value</th><th>Reading</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>(TP + TN) / N</td><td>965 / 1000 = <strong>96.5%</strong></td><td>looks great, but &quot;always predict legit&quot; scores 98%</td></tr><tr><td>Precision</td><td>TP / (TP + FP)</td><td>15 / 45 = <strong>33.3%</strong></td><td>only one in three flags is real</td></tr><tr><td>Recall (TPR, sensitivity)</td><td>TP / (TP + FN)</td><td>15 / 20 = <strong>75%</strong></td><td>catches three quarters of fraud</td></tr><tr><td>Specificity</td><td>TN / (TN + FP)</td><td>950 / 980 = 96.9%</td><td>FPR = 1 − specificity = 3.1%</td></tr><tr><td>F1</td><td>2PR / (P + R)</td><td><strong>0.46</strong></td><td>harmonic mean: punishes imbalance between P and R</td></tr><tr><td>F2 (recall-weighted)</td><td>(1 + β²)PR / (β²P + R), β = 2</td><td>0.60</td><td>when missing positives is costlier</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Move the threshold and watch the matrix, the metrics, and the ROC and PR points change:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"confusion","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-threshold-free-summaries\">Threshold-free summaries</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ROC curve</strong> plots TPR against FPR over all thresholds. <strong>ROC-AUC</strong> is the probability that a randomly chosen positive is ranked above a randomly chosen negative; 0.5 is random, 1.0 perfect. It is insensitive to class balance, which is also its weakness under heavy imbalance: the false-positive <em>rate</em> divides by the huge number of negatives, so many false positives barely move the curve.</li>\n<li><strong>PR curve</strong> plots precision against recall; <strong>PR-AUC (average precision)</strong> focuses on the positive class and <strong>falls when precision falls</strong>. Its baseline is the positive rate (0.02 for 2% fraud), not 0.5.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A demonstration with the same scoring quality (two normal score distributions 1.5 standard deviations apart): with 2% positives the ROC-AUC is 0.86 but the average precision is only <strong>0.20</strong> (the no-skill baseline for 2% positives is 0.02); with balanced classes the ROC-AUC is 0.85 and the average precision is <strong>0.85</strong>. The ROC-AUC barely changes; the PR-AUC reveals how hard the imbalanced problem really is.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Log loss</strong> <code>−mean[y log p + (1 − y) log(1 − p)]</code> penalises confident wrong probabilities; it evaluates both ranking and calibration.</li>\n<li><strong>Brier score</strong> is the mean squared error of probabilities; a <strong>calibration (reliability) curve</strong> plots predicted probability against observed frequency. Fix miscalibration with <strong>Platt scaling</strong> (logistic on the scores) or <strong>isotonic regression</strong> (<code>CalibratedClassifierCV</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>MCC</strong> (Matthews correlation coefficient) and <strong>Cohen&#39;s kappa</strong> give balanced single-number summaries robust to imbalance.</li>\n<li><strong>Multi-class averaging:</strong> <strong>macro</strong> (mean of per-class scores, each class equal), <strong>micro</strong> (pool all decisions), <strong>weighted</strong> (by class size).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-indian-bfsi-favourites-ks-gini-lift\">Indian BFSI favourites: KS, Gini, lift</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>KS statistic:</strong> the maximum gap between the cumulative distributions of scores for goods and bads; a value of 0.4 or more is typically good for a credit scorecard.</li>\n<li><strong>Gini = 2 × AUC − 1</strong> (so AUC 0.86 gives Gini 0.73).</li>\n<li><strong>Lift / gain / decile charts:</strong> how many times better than random the top deciles are at capturing the positives, used in marketing targeting (&quot;the top 10% of scores contain 3.1 times the average response rate&quot;).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.metrics <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> (confusion_matrix, precision_score, recall_score, f1_score,\n                             roc_auc_score, average_precision_score, classification_report)\ntn, fp, fn, tp = confusion_matrix(y_true, y_pred).ravel()        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># order: TN, FP, FN, TP</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(classification_report(y_true, y_pred))\nroc_auc_score(y_true, scores), average_precision_score(y_true, scores)   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># use scores/probabilities, not hard labels</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m8-classification-metrics~1","q":"Your fraud model has 99.5% accuracy. The fraud rate is 0.5%. Should the team celebrate?","a":"<p>With a 0.5% positive rate, a model that never flags anything scores 99.5% accuracy and catches zero fraud. Report <strong>recall</strong> (fraud caught), <strong>precision</strong> (share of flags that are real), <strong>PR-AUC</strong> and the confusion matrix at the chosen threshold, together with the operational cost (review capacity, loss per missed fraud). Compare with a baseline, and choose the threshold from costs.</p>\n","s":"No: predicting &quot;no fraud&quot; for everything also gives 99.5%; look at recall, precision and PR-AUC."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-classification-metrics~ca0_1","q":"Model flags 100 as fraud; 60 are fraud; there are 120 frauds in total. Precision and recall?","a":"<p>Precision 60/100 = 60%; recall 60/120 = 50%.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-classification-metrics~ca0_2","q":"99% of transactions are legitimate. A model predicting &#39;legit&#39; for all has what accuracy, precision and recall for fraud?","a":"<p>99% accuracy, undefined precision, 0% recall: accuracy is misleading on imbalanced data.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-classification-metrics~ca0_3","q":"Why does ROC AUC look optimistic on rare positives?","a":"<p>The false positive rate divides by the huge negative class, so many false alarms still give a small rate; precision-recall exposes it.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m8-classification-metrics~ta1_1","q":"<p>Using the same ten transactions with a threshold of 0.75, compute TP, FP, FN, precision, recall and accuracy.</p>\n","a":"<p>Above 0.75: the scores 0.95 (F), 0.90 (F) and 0.80 (L) are flagged. TP = 2, FP = 1, FN = 3 (the frauds at 0.70, 0.45 and 0.20), TN = 4. Precision = 2/3 ≈ 0.67; recall = 2/5 = 0.40; accuracy = (2 + 4) / 10 = 0.60. A high threshold gives the most trustworthy alerts and misses the most fraud.</p>\n"}]},"m8-metric-choice":{"id":"m8-metric-choice","slug":"choosing-the-metric-from-business-cost","m":8,"title":"Choosing the metric from business cost","min":20,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m8-classification-metrics"],"tags":["metrics","business"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Choosing evaluation metrics and thresholds from the business cost of errors, with worked expected-value calculations for fraud, churn and credit problems.","keyTerms":["cost-sensitive metrics","expected profit","threshold selection","fraud","churn","credit default","marketing lift"],"interviewQs":["Pick a metric and threshold strategy for fraud, churn and loan default, and defend it.","How do you turn error costs into a threshold?","Why might the best model by AUC not be the best for the business?"],"mistakes":["Choosing the metric first and the business goal second","Ignoring capacity constraints (review team size)","Using default thresholds"],"practice":"For a churn model with a ₹500 retention offer and ₹2,000 lifetime value at risk, compute the expected value at three thresholds.","related":["m8-classification-metrics","m13-problem-framing"],"points":"Problem - Costly error - Emphasize\nFraud detection - Missing fraud (FN) - Recall at acceptable precision; PR-AUC; review capacity\nSpam - Blocking a real email (FP) - Precision\nCancer screening - FN - Recall\nMarketing campaign - Budget wasted (FP) - Precision/lift at top deciles\nCredit default - Both (losses vs lost revenue) - KS, AUC, expected profit\nChurn retention offers - FP cost (discount) vs FN (lost customer) - Expected value with threshold tuning"},"intuitive":"<p>A metric is only useful if it measures what the business actually cares about. A spam filter that blocks a real email is worse than one that lets a spam through, so precision matters. A cancer screening that misses a case is worse than one that raises a false alarm, so recall matters. So before you pick &quot;F1&quot; or &quot;AUC&quot;, ask: what does each kind of mistake cost, in rupees, hours or trust? Then choose the threshold that makes the expected cost lowest.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> &quot;Which metric would you use, and why?&quot; tests whether you think about the decision, not only the model.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Start from error costs and capacity, then pick the metric and the threshold.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;I choose the metric from the cost of mistakes. Cancer screening: a missed case is terrible and a false alarm is cheap, so I maximise recall. Email spam filter: blocking a real email is worse than letting some spam through, so I favour precision. When both matter, F1 or a threshold chosen on the precision-recall curve. If errors have dollar costs, I compute expected cost directly: false negatives times cost plus false positives times cost, and pick the threshold that minimises it. For ranking tasks I use AUC or precision at K. The catch: I always say what the metric means for the business, and tune the threshold, not just the model.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Choose the threshold that costs least","html":"<p>Same ten transactions as topic 8.4. A missed fraud costs 500; a false alarm (a blocked genuine payment) costs 5.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Count the errors at each threshold:</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Threshold</th><th>FN</th><th>FP</th><th>Cost = 500 × FN + 5 × FP</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.50</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>1,000 + 10 = <strong>1,010</strong></td></tr><tr><td>0.35</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>500 + 15 = <strong>515</strong></td></tr><tr><td>0.15</td><td>0</td><td>4</td><td>0 + 20 = <strong>20</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><strong>Choose the cheapest:</strong> 0.15. When misses are 100 times more expensive than false alarms, flag generously.</li>\n<li><strong>Change the costs, change the answer.</strong> If a false alarm cost 200 (an angry customer and a manual review) and a miss cost 100: at 0.50, 200 + 400 = 600; at 0.35, 100 + 600 = 700; at 0.15, 0 + 800 = 800. Now <strong>0.50</strong> wins.</li>\n<li><strong>Explain it in business terms:</strong> &quot;At this threshold we catch every fraud in the sample for the price of four extra reviews.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Do it on validation data,</strong> then report the chosen threshold&#39;s precision and recall on the test set once.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The &quot;best&quot; metric and threshold come from what each mistake costs. Put the costs on the table, and the decision becomes arithmetic.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-course-table-with-reasoning","text":"The course table, with reasoning","level":3},{"id":"h-turning-costs-into-a-threshold","text":"Turning costs into a threshold","level":3},{"id":"h-capacity-constraints","text":"Capacity constraints","level":3},{"id":"h-fit-the-metric-to-the-question","text":"Fit the metric to the question","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Costly error</th><th>Emphasize</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fraud detection</td><td>Missing fraud (FN)</td><td>Recall at acceptable precision; PR-AUC; review capacity</td></tr><tr><td>Spam</td><td>Blocking a real email (FP)</td><td>Precision</td></tr><tr><td>Cancer screening</td><td>FN</td><td>Recall</td></tr><tr><td>Marketing campaign</td><td>Budget wasted (FP)</td><td>Precision/lift at top deciles</td></tr><tr><td>Credit default</td><td>Both (losses vs lost revenue)</td><td>KS, AUC, expected profit</td></tr><tr><td>Churn retention offers</td><td>FP cost (discount) vs FN (lost customer)</td><td>Expected value with threshold tuning</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-course-table-with-reasoning\">The course table, with reasoning</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Costly error</th><th>Emphasise</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fraud detection</td><td>Missing fraud (FN)</td><td>recall at acceptable precision, PR-AUC, review capacity</td><td>losses from fraud vs cost of reviewing flags</td></tr><tr><td>Spam</td><td>Blocking a real email (FP)</td><td>precision</td><td>users lose trust when real mail disappears</td></tr><tr><td>Cancer screening</td><td>FN</td><td>recall</td><td>a miss is far worse than a follow-up test</td></tr><tr><td>Marketing campaign</td><td>Wasted budget (FP)</td><td>precision or lift at the top deciles</td><td>only a fixed number of people can be contacted</td></tr><tr><td>Credit default</td><td>Both (losses vs lost revenue)</td><td>KS, AUC, expected profit</td><td>lending decisions trade off approvals and bad debt</td></tr><tr><td>Churn retention offers</td><td>FP cost (a discount) vs FN (lost customer)</td><td>expected value with threshold tuning</td><td>offers cost money; customers are worth money</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-turning-costs-into-a-threshold\">Turning costs into a threshold</h3>\n<p>If a flagged positive yields value <code>B</code> when correct (true positive) and costs <code>C</code> when wrong (false positive), act when <code>p · B − (1 − p) · C &gt; 0</code>, that is when <strong>p &gt; C / (B + C)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Churn example.</strong> A retention offer costs ₹500 and only pays off if the customer was going to churn (saving a customer worth ₹2,000 of future margin; assume the offer works on 50% of those who would have left, so expected benefit of a correct flag is ₹1,000 minus the ₹500 cost = ₹500 net, while a wrongly flagged loyal customer costs ₹500). Then <code>B = 500</code>, <code>C = 500</code>, and the rule is: contact customers with churn probability above 500 / (500 + 500) = <strong>0.5</strong>. If the offer costs only ₹100, then <code>B = 900</code>, <code>C = 100</code> and the threshold drops to <strong>0.10</strong>: contact many more. The right threshold depends on economics, not on 0.5.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected profit across thresholds</strong> (you can do this in a few lines):</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">expected_profit</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">y, p, thr, gain_tp, cost_fp</span>):\n    flag = p &gt;= thr\n    tp = (flag &amp; (y == <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>(); fp = (flag &amp; (y == <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>()\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> tp * gain_tp - fp * cost_fp\n\nthresholds = np.linspace(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.05</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.95</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">19</span>)\nbest = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">max</span>(thresholds, key=<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda</span> t: expected_profit(y_val, p_val, t, gain_tp=<span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>, cost_fp=<span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>))</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-capacity-constraints\">Capacity constraints</h3>\n<p>If the fraud team can review only 200 cases a day, choose the threshold that flags about 200, and compare models by <strong>precision@200 / recall@200</strong>, not by global AUC. For marketing with a budget for the top 10% of customers, use <strong>lift at the top decile</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-fit-the-metric-to-the-question\">Fit the metric to the question</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fraud:</strong> recall at a fixed false-positive rate, PR-AUC; add a rules layer for known patterns.</li>\n<li><strong>Churn:</strong> PR-AUC, lift at top 10%, expected profit after offer cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Credit default:</strong> KS, AUC/Gini, the approval-vs-loss trade-off (expected loss at the approved cut-off); report stability over time (PSI) and calibration.</li>\n<li><strong>Regression for ETA:</strong> MAE, P90 error, share of orders late by more than 10 minutes.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m8-metric-choice~1","q":"Two fraud models: Model A has higher ROC-AUC; Model B has higher precision at the top 200 alerts per day (the review capacity). Which do you deploy?","a":"<p>The team can only act on about 200 alerts a day, so the relevant quantity is how many true frauds sit in the top 200 (precision@200 or recall@200) and the money saved. Global AUC averages performance over thresholds you will never use. Check that B&#39;s advantage is stable (confidence intervals, different time periods) and that it is well-calibrated; then deploy B, while keeping A as a challenger in shadow mode.</p>\n","s":"Model B: performance at the operating point matters more than global ranking quality."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-metric-choice~ca0_1","q":"Missing a fraud costs $500, a false alarm costs $5. How do you pick a threshold?","a":"<p>Compute expected cost across thresholds, 500 × FN + 5 × FP, and choose the minimiser; it will lean towards high recall.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-metric-choice~ca0_2","q":"Which metric for a spam filter, and why?","a":"<p>Precision (or a high-precision operating point): wrongly blocking real mail costs more than letting some spam through.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-metric-choice~ca0_3","q":"Support can review only 50 flagged cases a day. Metric?","a":"<p>Precision at K = 50, since only the top 50 are actioned.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m8-metric-choice~ta1_1","q":"<p>A clinic can only follow up 3 flagged patients a week. A model ranks 10 patients, and the true positives are at ranks 1, 2, 5 and 9. Which metric should you report, and what is its value?</p>\n","a":"<p>With capacity for 3, report precision at 3: of the top 3 (ranks 1, 2, 3), two are true positives, so precision@3 = 2/3 ≈ 0.67. Recall@3 = 2/4 = 0.5 tells the clinic that half the positive patients are not followed up at this capacity, which is a useful argument for more resources.</p>\n"}]},"m8-other-metrics":{"id":"m8-other-metrics","slug":"regression-ranking-and-clustering-metrics","m":8,"title":"Regression, ranking and clustering metrics","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":[],"tags":["metrics","regression","ranking","clustering"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Metrics for regression, ranking and recommenders, and clustering: what each measures, when it misleads, and small numeric examples.","keyTerms":["MAE","RMSE","R-squared","MAPE","WAPE","precision at K","NDCG","MAP","MRR","silhouette"],"interviewQs":["MAE vs RMSE: which penalises large errors?","What is wrong with MAPE?","What is NDCG?","How do you evaluate a clustering with no labels?"],"mistakes":["Using MAPE when actuals can be near zero","Reporting R-squared alone","Judging a recommender by accuracy"],"practice":"Compute MAE, RMSE and WAPE for a small forecast table by hand.","related":["m8-classification-metrics","m9-time-series","m9-recommenders"],"points":"- MAE: average absolute error, robust, same units.\n- MSE/RMSE: penalizes large errors, RMSE in original units.\n- R^2: variance explained; can be negative on test.\n- MAPE / sMAPE / WMAPE: percentage errors (MAPE breaks near zero actuals).\n- Residual analysis always.\n\nPrecision@K, Recall@K, MAP, NDCG, MRR, hit rate, coverage, diversity.\n\nSilhouette, Davies-Bouldin, Calinski-Harabasz, and business interpretability."},"intuitive":"<p>Different jobs need different yardsticks. For predicting a number (delivery time), ask &quot;on average how many minutes off are we?&quot; (MAE), and whether big misses hurt more (RMSE). For recommending things, what counts is whether the good items appear near the top of the list (precision@K, NDCG). For grouping without labels, ask whether points are closer to their own group than to others (silhouette) and whether the groups make business sense.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Wrong metric, wrong model choice; interviews often ask &quot;how would you evaluate X?&quot;.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Match the metric to the output type: numbers, rankings or groups.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;For regression, MAE is the average absolute error, in the target&#39;s units and robust to outliers. MSE squares errors so large misses hurt more; RMSE returns to the original units. R squared is the share of variance explained, and can be negative on new data. MAPE is a percentage but breaks near zero. For ranking and recommenders: precision at K, recall at K, MAP and NDCG, which rewards putting relevant items near the top. For clustering without labels: silhouette score, from minus one to one, compares closeness to own cluster against the nearest other. The catch: I match the metric to how errors are felt, like RMSE when big misses are disproportionately bad.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"MAE, RMSE, MAPE and WAPE on one forecast","html":"<p>Four days of demand:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Actual</th><th>Forecast</th><th>Error</th><th>abs % error</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>100</td><td>110</td><td>+10</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>120</td><td>115</td><td>−5</td><td>4.2%</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>80</td><td>90</td><td>+10</td><td>12.5%</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>10</td><td>20</td><td>+10</td><td>100%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>MAE</strong> = (10 + 5 + 10 + 10) / 4 = <strong>8.75</strong> units: the typical miss, in units.</li>\n<li><strong>RMSE</strong> = √((100 + 25 + 100 + 100) / 4) = √81.25 ≈ <strong>9.0</strong>. A little higher than MAE because squaring weights bigger misses more.</li>\n<li><strong>MAPE</strong> = (10% + 4.2% + 12.5% + 100%) / 4 ≈ <strong>31.7%</strong>. Day 4 dominates: a miss of 10 units on a tiny day counts as 100%.</li>\n<li><strong>WAPE</strong> = total absolute error / total actual = 35 / 310 ≈ <strong>11.3%</strong>, a percentage that is not hijacked by small days.</li>\n<li><strong>Pick the one that matches the pain.</strong> Stock-outs cost in units: MAE. Big misses are disproportionately bad: RMSE. Executives want a percentage: WAPE, not MAPE.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Each error metric answers a slightly different question. MAPE misbehaves when actual values are near zero.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-8-5-regression","text":"Regression","level":2},{"id":"c-8-5-ranking-recommenders","text":"Ranking / recommenders","level":2},{"id":"c-8-5-clustering","text":"Clustering","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-regression","text":"Regression","level":3},{"id":"h-ranking-and-recommenders","text":"Ranking and recommenders","level":3},{"id":"h-clustering","text":"Clustering","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-8-5-regression\">Regression</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>MAE:</strong> average absolute error, robust, same units.</li>\n<li><strong>MSE/RMSE:</strong> penalizes large errors, RMSE in original units.</li>\n<li><strong>R^2:</strong> variance explained; can be negative on test.</li>\n<li><strong>MAPE / sMAPE / WMAPE:</strong> percentage errors (MAPE breaks near zero actuals).</li>\n<li>Residual analysis always.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-8-5-ranking-recommenders\">Ranking / recommenders</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Precision@K, Recall@K, MAP, NDCG, MRR, hit rate, coverage, diversity.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-8-5-clustering\">Clustering</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Silhouette, Davies-Bouldin, Calinski-Harabasz, and business interpretability.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-regression\">Regression</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Formula</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>MAE</strong></td><td>mean |y − ŷ|</td><td>same units, robust to outliers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MSE / RMSE</strong></td><td>mean (y − ŷ)²; its square root</td><td>penalises large errors more; RMSE in original units</td></tr><tr><td><strong>R²</strong></td><td>1 − SS_res / SS_tot</td><td>share of variance explained; can be <strong>negative</strong> on test data</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MAPE</strong></td><td>mean |y − ŷ| / |y|</td><td>percent errors; <strong>breaks near zero actuals</strong> (actual 0.1 predicted 1.0 gives 900%); biased toward under-forecasts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>sMAPE / WAPE</strong></td><td>sum of absolute errors / sum of actuals</td><td>WAPE is stable for retail demand with many small values</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Small example: actuals <code>[10, 20, 30]</code>, forecasts <code>[12, 18, 33]</code>. Errors are 2, 2, 3: MAE = 2.33; RMSE = √((4 + 4 + 9)/3) = 2.38 (slightly above MAE because of the larger miss); WAPE = 7 / 60 = 11.7%. If actuals were <code>[1, 50, 100]</code> MAPE would be dominated by the tiny first actual.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.metrics <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error, r2_score\nmean_absolute_error(y, p); mean_squared_error(y, p) ** <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.5</span>; r2_score(y, p)\nr2_score([<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>])        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># -1.5: predicting a constant that is not the mean is worse than the mean</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Always look at <strong>residual plots</strong> by segment and report percentile errors (P90) when tail errors matter, such as delivery promises.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-ranking-and-recommenders\">Ranking and recommenders</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Precision@K</strong>: fraction of the top K items that are relevant. <strong>Recall@K</strong>: fraction of all relevant items found in the top K.</li>\n<li><strong>MAP</strong> (mean average precision) averages precision at each relevant hit&#39;s rank. <strong>MRR</strong> (mean reciprocal rank): the average of 1 / rank of the first relevant item.</li>\n<li><strong>NDCG@K</strong> (normalised discounted cumulative gain): rewards relevant items, <strong>discounted by rank</strong> (a hit at position 1 counts more than one at position 10), and handles graded relevance; normalised to 1 for a perfect ordering.</li>\n<li><strong>Hit rate</strong>, <strong>coverage</strong> (share of the catalogue ever recommended), <strong>diversity</strong>, novelty. Online: CTR, conversion, GMV, retention, via A/B tests.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example: a list of 5 recommendations where items at positions 1, 3 and 5 are relevant. Precision@5 = 3/5 = 0.6; the first hit is at rank 1, so MRR = 1.0; NDCG discounts the ones at ranks 3 and 5.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-clustering\">Clustering</h3>\n<p>There are no labels, so combine measures: <strong>silhouette score</strong> (for each point, compare its average distance to its own cluster with the nearest other cluster; ranges from −1 to 1, higher is better), <strong>Davies-Bouldin</strong> (lower is better), <strong>Calinski-Harabasz</strong> (higher is better), plus <strong>stability</strong> across runs and <strong>business interpretability</strong> (can you name and act on each segment?).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m8-other-metrics~1","q":"Your demand forecast for thousands of slow-moving products has a MAPE of 400%, but the business says the forecasts look fine. What is going on?","a":"<p>MAPE divides by the actual, so products with actuals near zero produce huge percentage errors that dominate the average, even if the absolute errors are tiny. Use <strong>WAPE</strong> (total absolute error over total actuals), <strong>MAE</strong> and bias, evaluate at a meaningful aggregation (category, store), and weight by value. Also compare with a naive baseline to see the actual skill.</p>\n","s":"Many actuals are zero or tiny, so percentage errors explode; use WAPE, MAE, or a weighted metric."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-other-metrics~ca0_1","q":"Errors of 1, 1, 1, 9. MAE and RMSE?","a":"<p>MAE = 3; RMSE = √((1+1+1+81)/4) = √21 ≈ 4.6. RMSE is higher because it punishes the large miss.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-other-metrics~ca0_2","q":"Why is MAPE risky?","a":"<p>It explodes when actual values are near zero and is asymmetric between over- and under-forecasting.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-other-metrics~ca0_3","q":"What does NDCG reward that precision at K does not?","a":"<p>Position and graded relevance: relevant items higher in the list score more.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m8-other-metrics~ta1_1","q":"<p>A search engine returns three results with relevance 1, 0, 1 (1 = relevant). The ideal order would be 1, 1, 0. Compute DCG, the ideal DCG and NDCG, using a discount of 1/log₂(position + 1).</p>\n","a":"<p>DCG = 1/log₂2 + 0/log₂3 + 1/log₂4 = 1 + 0 + 0.5 = 1.5. Ideal DCG = 1/log₂2 + 1/log₂3 + 0 = 1 + 0.631 = 1.631. NDCG = 1.5 / 1.631 ≈ 0.92. Putting the second relevant result at position 3 instead of 2 cost about 8%.</p>\n"}]},"m8-imbalanced":{"id":"m8-imbalanced","slug":"imbalanced-data-playbook-and-self-check","m":8,"title":"Imbalanced data playbook and self-check","min":35,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m8-classification-metrics"],"tags":["imbalanced-data","classification","pitfalls"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The imbalanced data playbook: metrics, stratification, class weights, resampling (SMOTE) inside folds, threshold tuning and calibration, with a worked comparison.","keyTerms":["class weights","SMOTE","undersampling","resampling inside cross-validation","threshold tuning","PR-AUC","calibration","anomaly detection"],"interviewQs":["How do you handle a dataset with 1% positives?","Why must SMOTE be applied inside cross-validation?","Class weights vs resampling?","Why might probabilities be miscalibrated after resampling?"],"mistakes":["Oversampling before the train-test split","Reporting accuracy","Keeping the 0.5 threshold after rebalancing"],"practice":"Train a logistic regression with and without class weights on 2%-positive data and compare recall, precision and PR-AUC at several thresholds.","related":["m8-classification-metrics","m7-leakage","m5-bayes"],"points":"1. Don't use accuracy. Use PR-AUC, recall/precision at threshold, F1, cost-based metrics.\n2. Stratified splits and stratified CV.\n3. Class weights (class_weight='balanced', scale_pos_weight in XGBoost), often the first thing to try.\n4. Resampling: random undersampling, oversampling, SMOTE/ADASYN (apply only inside training folds, never before the split).\n5. Threshold tuning on validation using business costs.\n6. Anomaly-detection framing for extreme imbalance.\n7. Better features / more data for the minority class.\n8. Calibrate probabilities afterward (Platt, isotonic) if resampling distorts them.\n\n- Pick a metric and threshold strategy for fraud, churn, and loan default and defend it.\n- Explain why SMOTE must be applied inside cross-validation.\n- Build a leakage-free sklearn Pipeline with ColumnTransformer."},"intuitive":"<p>When only 1 in 100 cases is the thing you are hunting for, a lazy model that always says &quot;no&quot; is 99% accurate and completely useless. The fix has three parts: measure success with the right yardstick (not accuracy), tell the model that rare examples matter more (class weights) or show it more of them (resampling), and then choose the alert threshold carefully because the usual 50% cut-off no longer fits.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Fraud, churn, defaults and medical problems are all imbalanced; interviewers check you know the playbook.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Right metric, stratified splits, class weights first, resample only inside folds, tune the threshold.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;With imbalanced data, such as one per cent fraud, a model can get high accuracy by always predicting the majority. My playbook: first change the metric to precision, recall, PR-AUC. Use stratified splits so every fold has positives. Then try class weights, the simplest fix. Resample the training set only: undersample the majority, or oversample the minority, with SMOTE creating synthetic points. Tune the decision threshold. Consider anomaly detection if positives are extremely rare. The catch: resampling must happen after the split and only on training folds, otherwise duplicated rows leak into validation and inflate scores.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Class weights, by numbers","html":"<p>1,000 training transactions: 990 legitimate, 10 fraud.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The lazy model</strong> predicts &quot;legitimate&quot; for everything: accuracy 990 / 1,000 = <strong>99%</strong>, recall for fraud <strong>0%</strong>. Accuracy hides the failure completely.</li>\n<li><strong>Why it happens.</strong> Each legitimate row and each fraud row count equally in the loss, so getting the 990 right matters far more to the model than the 10.</li>\n<li><strong>Balanced class weights</strong> give each class equal total weight: weight = n / (number of classes × class count). Legitimate: 1,000 / (2 × 990) ≈ <strong>0.505</strong>. Fraud: 1,000 / (2 × 10) = <strong>50</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The effect.</strong> Each fraud now counts about 99 times as much as a legitimate row, and the two classes contribute equally to the loss. The model can no longer ignore fraud.</li>\n<li><strong>Then measure properly:</strong> precision, recall and PR-AUC, with a threshold chosen on validation data.</li>\n<li><strong>If you resample instead</strong> (oversampling or SMOTE), do it <em>inside</em> the training folds only, never before splitting.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> With rare positives, fix the metric first and then the training signal. Class weights are the simplest fix, with no data copied or invented.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-8-6","text":"8.6 Imbalanced data playbook","level":2},{"id":"c-8-7","text":"8.7 Self-check (Module 8)","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-playbook-in-order","text":"The playbook, in order","level":3},{"id":"h-a-worked-comparison-2-positives-20-000-rows","text":"A worked comparison (2% positives, 20,000 rows)","level":3},{"id":"h-why-smote-must-be-inside-cross-validation","text":"Why SMOTE must be inside cross-validation","level":3},{"id":"h-self-check","text":"Self-check","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-8-6\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.6</span> Imbalanced data playbook</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ol>\n<li>Don&#39;t use accuracy. Use PR-AUC, recall/precision at threshold, F1, cost-based metrics.</li>\n<li>Stratified splits and stratified CV.</li>\n<li><strong>Class weights</strong> (<code>class_weight=&#39;balanced&#39;</code>, <code>scale_pos_weight</code> in XGBoost), often the first thing to try.</li>\n<li><strong>Resampling:</strong> random undersampling, oversampling, <strong>SMOTE/ADASYN</strong> (apply only inside training folds, never before the split).</li>\n<li><strong>Threshold tuning</strong> on validation using business costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Anomaly-detection framing</strong> for extreme imbalance.</li>\n<li><strong>Better features / more data for the minority class.</strong></li>\n<li>Calibrate probabilities afterward (Platt, isotonic) if resampling distorts them.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> imblearn.pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> Pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> ImbPipeline\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> imblearn.over_sampling <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> SMOTE\npipe = ImbPipeline([(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;pre&#x27;</span>, pre), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;smote&#x27;</span>, SMOTE(random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">42</span>)), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;clf&#x27;</span>, clf)])</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-8-7\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.7</span> Self-check (Module 8)</h2>"},{"k":"cl","id":"m8-imbalanced.c1","kind":"self","items":["Pick a metric and threshold strategy for fraud, churn, and loan default and defend it.","Explain why SMOTE must be applied inside cross-validation.","Build a leakage-free sklearn Pipeline with ColumnTransformer."]},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-playbook-in-order\">The playbook, in order</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Do not use accuracy.</strong> Use PR-AUC, recall and precision at a threshold, F1/F2, and cost-based metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Stratified splits and stratified CV</strong> so each fold has positives.</li>\n<li><strong>Class weights</strong> (<code>class_weight=&quot;balanced&quot;</code>; <code>scale_pos_weight</code> in XGBoost): often the first and simplest thing to try; no data is altered.</li>\n<li><strong>Resampling:</strong> random undersampling of the majority (fast, loses information), random oversampling (duplicates), <strong>SMOTE/ADASYN</strong> (synthesises new minority points by interpolating between neighbours). <strong>Apply only inside the training folds, never before the split</strong>, or synthetic points derived from validation rows leak into training.</li>\n<li><strong>Threshold tuning</strong> on validation data using business costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Anomaly-detection framing</strong> when positives are extremely rare or unlabelled.</li>\n<li><strong>Better features / more data for the minority class</strong> often beats any resampling trick.</li>\n<li><strong>Calibrate</strong> probabilities afterwards (Platt, isotonic) if resampling or weights distorted them.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> imblearn.pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> Pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> ImbPipeline\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> imblearn.over_sampling <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> SMOTE\npipe = ImbPipeline([(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pre&quot;</span>, pre), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;smote&quot;</span>, SMOTE(random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">42</span>)), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;clf&quot;</span>, clf)])   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># SMOTE runs only on each training fold</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-worked-comparison-2-positives-20-000-rows\">A worked comparison (2% positives, 20,000 rows)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Logistic regression</th><th>Precision</th><th>Recall</th><th>PR-AUC</th><th>ROC-AUC</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>default (no weights), threshold 0.5</td><td>0.98</td><td>0.47</td><td>0.66</td><td>0.86</td></tr><tr><td><code>class_weight=&quot;balanced&quot;</code>, threshold 0.5</td><td>0.13</td><td>0.79</td><td>0.65</td><td>0.88</td></tr><tr><td>default model, <strong>threshold tuned for F1 (0.26)</strong></td><td>0.90</td><td>0.58</td><td>0.66</td><td>0.86</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Lessons: class weights <strong>change the operating point</strong> (recall up, precision collapses) but the ranking quality (PR-AUC) stays similar; the <strong>threshold</strong> is the real lever for the precision/recall balance; the weighted model&#39;s probabilities are inflated, so recalibrate if you need real probabilities. Choose the operating point from costs or review capacity, not from 0.5.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-why-smote-must-be-inside-cross-validation\">Why SMOTE must be inside cross-validation</h3>\n<p>If you oversample before splitting, synthetic minority points are built from neighbours that may end up in the validation fold, so the model has effectively seen the validation data: the score is inflated. A <code>Pipeline</code> from <code>imblearn</code> handles it per fold.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m8-imbalanced~1","q":"After using SMOTE the model&#39;s predicted probabilities average 0.4 while the real fraud rate is 1%. What do you do?","a":"<p>Resampling changes the class prior the model sees, so predicted probabilities no longer match real-world frequencies. If only the ranking matters (top-K review queues) this is fine. If you need probabilities (expected-loss calculations), <strong>recalibrate</strong> on a validation set drawn from the real distribution using Platt scaling or isotonic regression, or apply the prior correction <code>odds_real = odds_model × (π_real / (1 − π_real)) / (π_train / (1 − π_train))</code>.</p>\n","s":"Recalibrate (Platt or isotonic) on an untouched validation set, or rescale using the known prior."},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-self-check\">Self-check</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Pick a metric and threshold strategy for fraud, churn and loan default, and defend it.</li>\n<li>Explain why SMOTE must be applied inside cross-validation.</li>\n<li>Build a leakage-free sklearn Pipeline with ColumnTransformer.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-imbalanced~ca0_1","q":"Why must SMOTE be applied only inside the training fold?","a":"<p>Synthetic points derived from rows that also appear in validation leak information and make validation scores unrealistically high.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-imbalanced~ca0_2","q":"Cheapest first fix for imbalance?","a":"<p>Class weights (or adjusting the threshold) with a proper metric; no data changes needed.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m8-imbalanced~ca0_3","q":"After oversampling, predicted probabilities are inflated. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>Choose the threshold on an untouched validation set or recalibrate to the true base rate.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m8-imbalanced~ta1_1","q":"<p>A colleague applies SMOTE to the full dataset (creating synthetic fraud cases) and then does a train/validation split. Validation recall jumps from 0.55 to 0.92. Why is that suspicious?</p>\n","a":"<p>SMOTE creates synthetic frauds by interpolating between real fraud rows. After the split, many validation frauds have near-twins, made from the same originals, in the training set, so the model recognises them almost by memory. The jump is leakage, not skill. Split first, apply SMOTE only to the training part (for example with an <code>imblearn</code> pipeline inside cross-validation), and evaluate on untouched real data.</p>\n"}]}});